{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1050 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "ee8a7fe9cc3ca2abb2648afad83ac5f76d640d7a3f75a1e655d48e2e0c946c5d" :edb/db-before-t 1049 :edb/db-after-t 1050 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534362 50 #inst "2026-10-06T17:06:18.800Z" 13194139534362 true] [17592186105383 1100 "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,2), (0,1)$ and $(1,1)$. Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S'$, the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$?\n\n1. $(-2,0)$\n2. $(0,0)$\n3. $(2,0)$" 13194139534362 false] [17592186105383 1100 "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,2), (0,1)$ and $(1,1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S',$ the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf T?$\n\n- $(-2,0)$\n\n- $(0,0)$\n\n- $(2,0)$" 13194139534362 true] [17592186105383 1145 "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nTX &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-2,0)$, $(0,0)$, and $(2,0)$.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139534362 false] [17592186105383 1145 "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-2,0),$ $(0,0),$ and $(2,0).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II,  and III.\"" 13194139534362 true] [17592186105384 1100 "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ Find the image of the triangle shown below under the action of $\\mathbf T$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/71b4c00c3f3f2354e52c4ddadc41fdd1.png)" 13194139534362 false] [17592186105384 1100 "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n  Find the image of the triangle shown below under the action of $\\mathbf T.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/853fd7d5c09944a5bacd0c2b3453504f3fa1eea9d8d3b2c3bdd4f0f44889dc16.png)" 13194139534362 true] [17592186105384 1145 "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(3,-6), (1,-2),$ and $(-2,4).$  Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/3fd1073b2eaa5aa9c21e537fcc4dc6cf538ab2992c0732f64da50dbcb63d8fd4.png)" 13194139534362 true] [17592186105384 1145 "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nTX &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(3,-6), (1,-2)$, and $(-2,4)$. Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/90a937e06df1fa3177ee4608a88110e5.png)" 13194139534362 false] [17592186105385 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/89efcd0ecb51dac08a1102c5a347eea3459f7209de7cbac0a88b192259ca3b7e.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $\\mathbf{T}?$" 13194139534362 true] [17592186105385 1100 "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/66b3a70a542c03540cf219bdce0d024d.png)" 13194139534362 false] [17592186105385 1145 "Recall that for any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with associated $2 \\times 2$ matrix $T$, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., $\\det(T) \\neq 0$, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., $\\det(T) = 0$, we have two additional subcases:If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0)$, the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the origin." 13194139534362 false] [17592186105385 1145 "Recall that for any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with associated $2 \\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., $\\det(T) \\neq 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., $\\det(T) = 0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the origin." 13194139534362 true] [17592186105818 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284110 13194139534362 true] [17592186105818 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284117 13194139534362 true] [17592186105818 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284159 13194139534362 true] [17592186105818 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284166 13194139534362 true] [17592186105819 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284103 13194139534362 true] [17592186105819 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284145 13194139534362 true] [17592186105819 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284152 13194139534362 true] [17592186105819 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284173 13194139534362 true] [17592186105820 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284124 13194139534362 true] [17592186105820 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284131 13194139534362 true] [17592186105820 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284138 13194139534362 true] [17592186105992 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534362 true] [17592186105992 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/e2ab6906100a96a123b4b1520613f1e38fe2c7e77af390a8efe0596a2861727d.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" 13194139534362 true] [17592186105992 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/q-162145.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T$?" 13194139534362 false] [17592186105992 1145 "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-3)-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. Therefore, the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a line segment." 13194139534362 true] [17592186105994 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534362 true] [17592186105994 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/e6d0ce147aec90c3840a893e36596fbadf981ff96559f65d055f4486406cca7f.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T?$" 13194139534362 true] [17592186105994 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/q-162206.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T$?" 13194139534362 false] [17592186105994 1145 "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a nonsingular matrix. Therefore, the image of our object under the action of  $T$ is a quadrilateral." 13194139534362 true] [17592186106081 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534362 true] [17592186106081 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/5c0196b13b3f20ba05313a9604e73b13251937b525a12fd5b9997e99f4ae21a0.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" 13194139534362 true] [17592186106081 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/q-21061.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T$?" 13194139534362 false] [17592186106081 1145 "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-1,1)$, $(1,-1),$ $(-1,1).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $T$ is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/2f8d6ec6c9a71e61180ad04c25d623c40dadfdd623e1e2eaa8458915bcaca2f8.png)" 13194139534362 true] [17592186106082 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534362 true] [17592186106082 1100 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,-1), (0,-1)$, and $(0, 0)$. Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S^{′}$, the image of $S$ under the action of $T$?\n\n1. $(4,-1)$\n2. $(0, 0)$\n3. $(-4,-8)$" 13194139534362 false] [17592186106082 1100 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,-1),(0,-1),$ and $(0,0).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$\n\n- $(4,-1)$\n- $(0,0)$\n- $(-4,-8)$" 13194139534362 true] [17592186106082 1145 "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-4,-8),$ $(-1,-2)$, and $(0,0).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534362 true] [17592186284103 1017 #edb/ref 17592186284104 13194139534362 true] [17592186284103 1089 "q-147649" 13194139534362 true] [17592186284103 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534362 true] [17592186284103 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/57941a3ed467617de9d41a2d8239b75060ba90b7a8c8980e5dabd3afb0be8b6b.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" 13194139534362 true] [17592186284103 1113 #uuid "7076e203-2050-53c1-bc3c-efab42cd73e9" 13194139534362 true] [17592186284103 1145 "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-1,1)$, $(-5,5),$ $(-3,3).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $T$ is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/cf336b4522756131aeb6dd9bc48ada6f8b255526b6ae20120dcf40f155b1b95c.png)" 13194139534362 true] [17592186284104 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284105 13194139534362 true] [17592186284104 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284106 13194139534362 true] [17592186284104 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284107 13194139534362 true] [17592186284104 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284108 13194139534362 true] [17592186284104 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284109 13194139534362 true] [17592186284104 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534362 true] [17592186284104 1041 "selection" 13194139534362 true] [17592186284104 1104 #edb/ref 17592186284107 13194139534362 true] [17592186284104 1110 #uuid "1160794a-84c7-5140-bbc1-ded3cd0dfaa4" 13194139534362 true] [17592186284105 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/4966000e14a3ed40